            "The World Health Organization's <strong>AWaRe</strong> classification sorts antibiotics into three groups that Ghana's Standard Treatment Guidelines and Essential Medicines List now follow. <strong>Access</strong> antibiotics — amoxicillin, amoxicillin–clavulanate, ampicillin, benzylpenicillin, cloxacillin, gentamicin, metronidazole, doxycycline, cotrimoxazole, nitrofurantoin, chloramphenicol — are the narrow, affordable first choices for the common infections and should make up at least sixty per cent of a facility's consumption. <strong>Watch</strong> antibiotics — ceftriaxone, cefuroxime, cefixime, ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin, azithromycin, clarithromycin, vancomycin, piperacillin–tazobactam, meropenem — have higher resistance potential and are reserved for the specific indications in the guideline, which is why a ward where every patient is on ceftriaxone has a stewardship problem, not a sicker population. <strong>Reserve</strong> antibiotics — colistin, linezolid, the newer beta-lactam combinations — are last resorts for confirmed multidrug-resistant infections, held under restriction. The classification gives the nurse a simple test: when a Watch or Reserve drug is written up, the indication should be written beside it.",
            "The laboratory speaks first through the <strong>Gram stain</strong>, available within an hour wherever there is a microscope: <strong>Gram-positive</strong> organisms hold the crystal violet and stain purple — <em>Staphylococcus</em> in clusters, <em>Streptococcus</em> in chains, <em>Clostridium</em> as spore-forming rods — and are broadly the penicillin-susceptible group; <strong>Gram-negative</strong> organisms lose the violet and stain pink — <em>E. coli</em>, <em>Klebsiella</em>, <em>Salmonella</em>, <em>Shigella</em>, <em>Pseudomonas</em>, <em>Vibrio</em>, and <em>Neisseria</em> as paired cocci — and carry in their outer membrane the endotoxin that causes septic shock. A Gram stain of cerebrospinal fluid showing Gram-negative diplococci names the meningococcus before the culture grows; Gram-positive cocci in clusters from a wound name the staphylococcus and raise the question of MRSA. The <strong>culture and sensitivity report</strong> follows in two to three days: the organism, and against each drug tested <strong>S</strong> (sensitive — standard doses will work), <strong>I</strong> (intermediate — may work at high dose or at a site where the drug concentrates) or <strong>R</strong> (resistant), sometimes with the minimum inhibitory concentration. The nurse reads it as closely as the prescriber: a patient on ceftriaxone whose <em>E. coli</em> is reported resistant to it and sensitive to gentamicin and nitrofurantoin is a patient whose treatment must change today, and a report that arrives on a Friday evening and sits in a tray until Monday is a report that has killed people. Specimens collected before the first dose, labelled fully, transported promptly and chased for results are the nurse's half of the laboratory partnership; and where the laboratory cannot help, the <strong>local antibiogram</strong> — the hospital's own summary of which organisms resist which drugs — guides the empirical choice better than habit.",
